Guru;
  I have been using the IP Aliasing now for 5 years in a simulation
environment. There are two situations:
1) You develop the application. In that case you use the desired IP
address in the bind call of the socket. The normal value of '0' that you
see in the books tells the OS to pick the best interface. By specifying
what you want, that is what you get if it is available.

2) A process you don't control (e.g. telnet) will use the '0' and let
the OS use the best IP based upon the destination and the current
routing knowledge. You basicly can't force it.....

