Wow - a customer who gets tcpdump traces without prodding/hand holdling.
What's the world coming to?

>getport prog "netlock"  V3  prot UDP  port 0 (ttl 32, id 37915)
>17:30:00.332431 barbie.ukdcs.co.uk.24c875bf > 172.19.46.83.pmap-v2: 28
>reply getport 2098 (ttl 30, id 50593)
>17:30:00.347079 172.19.46.83.24c875c9 > barbie.ukdcs.co.uk.netlock-v3: 60
>call free_all name "E:computer" state 0 (ttl 32, id 38171)
>17:30:01.350985 172.19.46.83.24c875c9 > barbie.ukdcs.co.uk.netlock-v3: 60
>call free_all name "E:computer" state 0 (ttl 32, id 38427)
>17:30:02.341220 172.19.46.83.24c875c9 > barbie.ukdcs.co.uk.netlock-v3: 60
>call free_all name "E:computer" state 0 (ttl 32, id 38683)
>17:30:03.341220 172.19.46.83.24c875c9 > barbie.ukdcs.co.uk.netlock-v3: 60
>call free_all name "E:computer" state 0 (ttl 32, id 38939)

So, rpc.lockd isn't replying.  Could be that it's busy trying to resolve
a hostname with the name server, but it could also be trying to talk to
rpc.statd.  Statd could be hung up trying to talk to a machine that
went down is won't be coming back.  Take a look at /etc/sm and /etc/sm.bak.
Those are directories with entries that are hostnames of monitored nodes.
If any of those nodes are gone, delete their entries and restart statd and
lockd.

You may also want to try running them with "-d1", but the results can
be voluminous and disruptive if your server is busy.
