Mario Zimmermann wrote:
> 
> Hello
> 
> we are evaluation a migration from VMS to Digital Unix.
> We will share the disks of a server over NFS.
> The clients will have Unix local on their disks.
> 
> I'm now searching for the best/fastest way to install/setup the clients.
> Do i have to install Unix from CD-Rom on every client?
> Can i boot from CD-Rom, mount a networkdisk with a sample installation
> and simply copy it from there?
> Are there other ways?
> 
> Thanks for your help.
> 
> Regards
> 
> Mario
> 
> e-mail: mario.zimmermann@pcm.bosch.de

The most convenient way to set up lots of (nearly) identical clients
ist to set up an RIS area (Remote Installation Service). There
you can define what software (including the base OS of course) is
to be installed on each client. The clients can be booted and set up
via network/mop. If the machines to be set up are indeed identical
you can just setup a reference machine (maybe this one running the
ris-server) and simply copy most of the config files in /etc.
Only a few of (really) machine specific entries must be kept such as
hostname or IP address. If all the machines have the same hardware
equipment you even don't need to care about partitioning, swap
configuration and so on.

Hope this gives a hint.

/knut




