A slower solution if you cannot use RIS ( I can't have a clear answer
from our DEC commercial if it need a license or not).

You can make floppy which contain exactly the same files than the RIS 
server and boot on the CDROM with the floppy in the drive. In this case
the installation won't ask you anything and you can make specific action
before the installation and after with scripts. 

In my case, the first script make the good partition scheme, then the 
software is installed, the kernel is compiled, and after the reboot
the second script is launch that configure the network, install the 
licenses and mount a disk from a NFS then launch the install script on this
disk that install the development and test environnement !

It's longer than RIS, you need to put the CD-ROM in each station,
you need to well prepare your floppy ( but you'll have the same to do for
the RIS server ), but it's free and it permit me to install 40 stations in three days with 4 DU CD's.

Beware that this method is possible only for Digital Unix 4.0b and greater.
This method is well documented in an appendix of the installation book.

And if you have trouble with this, feel free to mail me or to post here.
