(Kathleen A Ferraro) writes:
>
>We are getting a lot of
>server (not web server)  crashes and some other aberrant behavior
>

I can't tell you for certain if this is your problem, but it very well may
be.  When you start Netscape, you X Display Server (the software running on
your X window terminal) is told that the entire Netscape window is "hot" (a
term I invented for lack of having a better term).  By "hot", I mean, the X
Display Server software continuously updates the X Host Client (your alpha
box) with the position of the pointer.

Yes, **CONTINUOUSLY**  !!

This is very apparent if you have network sniffer.  Plug it in, and turn on
the "audio" (many sniffer, will give a geiger counter like click for each
packet that goes by).  If the pointer is outside of the Netscape window
very few clicks will happen (cursor blink on dxterm or xterm is actually
done by drawing a black box and then a white box; turn off blink and watch
the network traffic go down).  When resting inside a Netscape window that
is "in focus", the rate will be much higher.  Slowly drag (with no buttons
pushed) across the Netscape window and you geiger counter will go nuts !!!

Now, if you network is not up to the flood of packets that this causes, you
have a problem !!

Worst of all, this "hot window" behavior exists on the CDE Toolbar that is
standard equipment in V4.0.

I've talked to DEC and they say that's the way it is.  If somebody knows
different please tell me.

Jack Patteeuw

