Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
References: <69m85g$r59@nntp02.primenet.com> <69ocr9$jj9@emily.oit.umass.edu>
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: Digital Equipment Corp., Software Partners Engineering
Mime-Version: 1.0
Reply-To: Jeff.Donsbach@digital.com
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


If I remember correctly, the Personal Workstations do not support
external drives. There is a connector
on the back of the SCSI card and you can physically plug it in, but it's
not supported.

The reason has to do with maximum cable lengths. The UltraSCSI spec has
some restrictions on maximum
cable lengths on one SCSI bus and apparently the internal cables inside
the box already are pretty much
at that limit.

I'm not a hardware engineer, but that's what I remember reading here in
an internal Notes file.
Check with your normal Digital support channels on this.

If I'm right, and you happen to get it to work anyway, be aware you are
on your own. On a heavily loaded
PCI bus, you could see data corruption. BEWARE!

At one time, there were also some restrictions on adding a second SCSI
adapter to these systems, but
I think they might have been lifted on newer systems. Again, check with
your Digital sales/support rep to
make sure.

Hope this helps,
-Jeff D.


