Few months ago, I tried to obtain the smallest kernel for my hosts.  I 
went on stripping, and removing ATM related features we don't need,
etc, to have it decrease its size (nearly 14Mb with GENERIC features).

All of what I have done is because someone told me that the DU kernel
was a dynamic one.  I thought of modules you can had or remove when
needed, like Solaris for example.  So I tried to configure the
[/sys/conf/files] file where I saw:

[munch]
MODULE/STATIC/cm                standard Binary
bsd/cm_cfg.c                    module cm
[munch]
MODULE/DYNAMIC/atmip                    optional atmip Binary
netinet/atm/atm_arp.c              module atmip optimize -g3
netinet/atm/ip_converge.c          module atmip optimize -g3
[munch]

I thought I could just replace STATIC when I don't need a module all
the time to DYNAMIC and that's it.  But it didn't worked.  I think
it's because not all modules are programmed in a way to make then
either STATIC or DYNAMIC.

So I though of "dynamic" in another way.  What if the kernel is dynamic
in the way of "No need to reboot to configure the XXX kernel
parameter".  I thought of the sysconfig command with which you can set
parameters dynamically.

I'd like to have your opinions on the subject:

Does DU 
	- have a dynamically parametrable kernel,
