Joe Pasko <pasko@scri.fsu.edu> wrote:
> Am I running up against a system limit ? (Cabeling shouldn't(?) be a
> problem as all 4 disks are in a 4pack and I'm using a 3ft cable.)

My experience is that regardless of how far you are from the official SCSI
cable length limits, if you start seeing flakeyness of the sort you've
described, cable problems are what you should be suspecting.

PCI SCSI cards are fairly cheap.  You may just want to put another SCSI
bus on the system to dedicate to your 4-pack disk array.  It could save
you a lot of hair-tearing-out, and you'll get better performance that way
too (even with a relatively low-performance SCSI card, just the fact that
you've put your system and data disks on separate busses will be a win).

If you don't want to go that route, here's something to try.  Get one of
those Apple Macintosh feed-through terminators and put it in the *middle*
of the SCSI chain, in addition to the terminators at each end.  It's ugly,
it violates all sorts of specs, it shouldn't work, and your system people
may laugh at the idea, but I've seen it do the trick.  Call it black magic
if you want, but it's worth a try.  If it doesn't work, you're not any
worse off for the attempt.  But the 2nd SCSI card is a better idea :-)

Me?  I'm eagerly awaiting FireWire to replace all this SCSI crap.

