We are in the process of gathering information for upgrading a
computer lab for students that today consists of total of 20 boxes of
various makes - Sun SS10, HP715/50 and HP712/60.

We use this lab for the following applications:

* CAD (I-DEAS and Pro/Engineer)
* FEM (LS-Dyna, Ansys, in hous apps)
* Software development (mainly fortran)
* General purpouse analytic and numerical calculations (Matlab,
  Mathematica and Maple)
* Documentation software (Frame and others)

We are generally very pleased with our Alphas when numerical
performance is concerned - we use for instance two memorychannel-
clustered AS4000:s for heavy FEM jobs and they run wonderfully.

For this lab, we have set up some base requirements:

* Reasonably fast
* 24 bit graphics, preferably with some kind of OpenGL acceleration

Sun and SGI are sort of setting the reference in this segment -
especially the Sun U170 with the Creator 3D graphics make a really
juicy choice if you look at the overall price/performance ratio. In
addition to this Sun is rolling out a new line of inexpensive Ultras
in mid-january, with a 270 MHz UltraSparc II system in some
configuration priced at $5k list. Admittedly, there is still no CPU
from Sun or SGI that can match a fast Alpha, but when you look at the
price...

HP seems to be out of consideration, saying flat-out that "in this
price segment we compete with our NT workstations" (oh, yes, this is a
quote from my HP sales rep). *puke*

How is it with Digital? This far we haven't seen anything that can
match Sun or Digital in that price range from where I'm standing, all
rebates applied. Is there something in the making or is Digital
assuming a position like HP? The "NT will kill Digital Unix.
Resistance is futile. You will be assimilated." campaign that Digital
had last year didn't feel all that reassuring.

Are there any probable rumors at least about products from Digital
that would match my specs?

Thanks,
/Erik

