John Kohl wrote:

> Ok, you asked for it...  (warning, flame-bait follows :)
> Why is -std1 not the default mode of operation (or at least -std)?
> C has been standardized for 8 years now, shouldn't the default mode of
> the compiler, without extra flags, be the standard behavior?  Let those
> who still insist on non-standard K&R C provide the command line flag(s).

Coming soon to a compiler near you...

The default mode for DEC C in DIGITAL UNIX V5.0 will be -std (relaxed
ANSI).
This will give you the checking and features that "well behaved" (ANSI)
source
code will benefit from in addition to some common extensions to make the
code easier to compile/run on many platforms. You would be surprised at
the
amount of source code out there that is not ANSI compliant, even to this
day.

You can, however, change your default in previous versions of DEC C by
editing
the comp.config file in /usr/lib/cmplrs/cc. See man cc(1) for more info.

-Jeff
