I received the following from a colleague when asked about
this problem:

> This is very likely a problem with their process limits.
> They need to first bump their stack limits for the process and
> if that's not enough, bump the system limits in /etc/sysconfigtab.

You should be able to check this using the "limit" command in
the C-shell. The default stacksize may be around 2048 kbytes.

Try to do a "limit stacksize unlimited" in your shell and then
re-run the (crashing) application. You could then experiment with
different values. See man csh(1) and search for "limit" or
man ulimit(1) for more information.

If this works, then the quickest fix might be to write a shell
"wrapper" that bumps up the stacksize and then invokes the program.
The change should affect only that shell. For example:

% ulimit -s
2048

% cat t.csh
#! /bin/csh
ulimit -s
limit stacksize unlimited
ulimit -s

% t.csh
2048
32768
%  ulimit -s
2048

> They will also need to link with raised -T and -D limits to relocate
> the program  in an address space that can accomodate the larger stack.
> The default limits are -T 120000000 and -D 140000000.  They can
> be bumped to -T 200000000 and -D 220000000.

> Anyone using large stacks has to address raising all these limits.


Let us know if any of this helps.

Thanks,
-Jeff
