In <t067nx34qh.fsf@sibelius.ikp.liu.se> Erik Persson wrote:
> 
> Am I mistaking about these features? Have I gotten a totally wrong
> idea about configuring the Digital Unix kernel? I think the way Sun
> has done it is pretty nice, and HP seems to have done the same thing
> with HP-UX 11.
> 
> Comments and answers would be very appreciated.

Mostly correct.  In no way are you recompiling the kernel when you do
a 'doconfig.'  Compiling osf takes considerably more than the 1.5 - 10
minutes doconfig requires.  Mostly, it's compiling static data sources,
stuffs them into a compressed archive format, and then the whole mass
is linked into a monolithic kernel.  This part is, indeed, like old bsd.

The sad part is that while Sun and HP went from monolithic to modular,
DU went from modular-for-free (osf/1) to monolithic.  That said, the
mach 2.5 mig (mach interface generator) code that was part of building
the module interfaces didn't produce fast mechanisms for moving data
between user and kernel modes.  It was very programmer-friendly (having
done loadable kernel services on NeXTstep) but more overhead than fixed
syscalls and statically-bound code.

The current trend *is* to re-modularize, however.  More and more stuff
will be loadable and unloadable.  Legacy code and legacy buses just make
this hard in some cases.

m
