Ron wrote:

> Hi,
>
> Does anyone know if rumba has been ported to Dec Unix 3.2 (rumba is the
> software that enables one to mount Windows exported disks on Unix) ?
>
> If yes, could you give me the URL for the sources/binaries for that port
> ?
> If no, could you give me the URL for gcc's port to Dec Unix 3.2 (so that
> I may attampt the port myself) ?
>
> Thanks,
> Ron.

There are some important points that appear to becoming lost in this
discussion following on from Ron's question.

There are two software packages involved.  Samba is the base which provides
a Miscrosoft networking-like environment for other types of machines, say
Unix.  Samba works well with DUnix with a port of the software included in
the standard distribution.  Rumba is a separate package that provides a
NFS-type facility to Unix machines using the Samba environment.  Ports to
three or four Unix environments are now included in the standard
distribution of Rumba (version 0.5b) but a port to DUnix is NOT included.
The advantage I see for using Rumba is that NFS server software is not
required for the Microsoft machines.

I have been attempting to port Rumba to DUnix but currently without
success.  The stumbling point is compatibility between the socket protocols
that can be defined to perform the network communications and that used by
DUnix when implementing the mount() system call.  The documentation
available on the mount() call has been of no assistance.  Without solving
this problem of using the mount() system call a port of Rumba cannot
proceed.  I would be willing to provide my efforts in porting Rumba to DUnix
if somebody can show me a code segment that successfully implements a
mount() system call for NFS.  This appears to me to be the root of this
porting problem.

Ross Maloney   x409@cs.curtin.edu.au
Graduate Student
Curtin University of Technology




