>  You found the fix.  I know this isn't much help, but I had a
>similar problem with a DUX 3.2g system NFS serving gigabytes of 10K+
>users home directories in a mixed-platform environment.

Details?  jreali seems to be describing two problems, one with accessing
links, one with reading directories.  READDIRPLUS is a NFS request introduced
in NFS V3.  That's where the NetApp problem lay.  An easy, but heavy-handed,
way to disable READDIRPLUS is to mount with the "noac" option.  You won't
want to run that way as it will cause quite a bit more NFS traffic, but
it makes for a convenient test.

For chasing link issues, our development environment is symlink crazy, and
I wrote this script to help chase down missing mounts:

#!/bin/csh -f
# fstep - follow a pathname's components to the final file.

set link = $1
set list = ()
@ i = 1
newlink:
echo $link | grep -q '^/'
if ($status == 0) then
	cd /
endif
set new = ( `echo $link | tr '/' ' '` )
set list = ( $new $list[$i-] )
@ i = 1
while ( $i <= $#list )
	set ls = `ls -lgd $list[$i]`
	echo $ls
	if ( $#ls == 11 ) then
		@ i = $i + 1
		set link = $ls[11]
		goto newlink
	else
		if (-d $list[$i]) cd $list[$i]
		@ i = $i + 1
	endif
end


