In article <casper.884795241@uk-usenet.uk.sun.com>,
Casper H.S. Dik - Network Security Engineer <Casper.Dik@Holland.Sun.Com> wrote:
>
>yes, it would be nice if all the Unix systems would use the same
>common binary interface so that vendors only need to supply one
>unix binary instead of 10.

There was a time when this was almost reality. There was one 
common, popular, open standard for binary formats in UNIX, 
and almost everyone followed it. 
Then UNIX became increasingly commercialized and one of the most basic
failings of the commercial software industry had its field day:
you can't make money on something that is free, so make something else
that is not compatible and charge for it. 
As a result, all the license-paying SysV clones came out with more or
less incompatible formats. 
And of course adacemia has produced a fair variety of formats which some
folks became convinced were all wonderful, and then went out into the
industry and used, and thus added to the incompatibility. 

Though the UNIX world still enjoys a _vast_ amount of freedom in the standards
department ( rather to say that they still follow mostly free standards and
license-free things at the second tier), and the rest of the software
world is a LOT more closed and controlled than the Unix world. 
But, no, you do not have a common executable format anymore.
And at no time was there binary compatibility across different
architectures! (well, except for DEC's FX!32 and the PDP-11 compatibility 
on VAXen) (unless IBM's VM qualifies too) Never in the PeeCee world.

>
>Of course, the Unix market leader may not care about that and the
>Free unixes will be prompt in adding emulators for the most important
>commercial offerings.

this is a good point- NetBSD has a strong support for binaries of 
other operating systems (wihtin an architecture) for example.


If people refuse to keep bowing down to wintel, then maybe something
can be done. But if people just admit defeat and give up without a
fight, then there really is no hope. 


But the main idea that was so big in the UNIX world, especially until about
2 or 3 years ago, was that software was available in *source code*. 
Then, it really WAS portable to almost anything. You compiled it on your
own system which might have its own quirks which your compiler can handle.

Porting is still an issue, but software written to be portable, will generally
work without too much of a port. 

one of the few good things POSIX might have actually done.. ;)

and of course remmeber and follow the old adage:
"never trust a program you don't have the source to!"


happy hacking,
Isildur




