What the f* is this Duane guy talking about? _We_ have to rebuild kernels
whenever we move controllers.

duane@hunch.zk3.dec.com (Andrew Duane USG/PE) writes:

> 
> In article <t067nx34qh.fsf@sibelius.ikp.liu.se> Erik Persson <erik@ikp.liu.se> writes:
> >I wonder a bit about the modularity and methods for reconfiguration
> >for the Digital Unix kernel.
> >
> >As opposed to the SunOS 5 kernel, which is organized as a barebones
> >bootstrap with virtually all subsystems and drivers provided as binary
> >module files that can be loaded (and unloaded) upon request, the
> >Digital Unix still looks and feels very much like a 4.old BSD kernel
> >
> >I find it pretty stoneage that the kernel has to be _recompiled_ and
> >rebuilt every time kernel components are added or updated. Why does it
> >have to be like that? I might be mistaking, but there does indeed seem
> >to be mechanisms and tools for Digital Unix providing unloading and
> >loading kernel modules and I even think that I saw it being used once
> >- when I installed the tape changer dirvers (mc driver), eventough I
> >still didn't get around recompiling the kernel. 
> 
> 
> We have several such features already (a few in V3.2* and many more
> in V4.0*). 
> 
> The primary feature is kloadsrv, which is used to dynamically load
> and unload kernel modules. There are other features such as
> bootstrap linking which can assemble a kernel "on-the-fly" at
> bootstrap time. 
> 
> Most/all of the disk subsystem is now completely dynamic; you
> do not need to recompile to add/remove/rename disks, controllers,
> etc.
> 
> Additionally, as more and more layered products and drivers
> move to the new dynamic loading scheme, there will be less
> and less to compile, and less need to rebuild a kernel at all.
> 
> Digital Unix is moving away from the old "compile" strategy for
> kernels, but has been hampered by several obstacles, including
> the need to support backward compatibility, and the need to
> handle 24 different platforms with dozens of different hardware
> and bus types.
> 
> 
